# Naming and Single-Responsibility Audit: Independent Findings, Method and Defects
File: openOODA/audits/naming/devin.oot

Provenance: converted from `openOODA/audits/naming-audit-devin.md` (283 lines). Part 1 of 2.

# Naming & Single-Responsibility Audit — Independent Findings (Devin)

**Scope:** All repositories across the 9 openOODA organizations under `/Projects/` (read-only inspection only: directory names,
`README.md`, `Makefile` `BIN`/`PERSONAS`, `.oo` page inventories, governing docs). No compilers, builds, or tests were executed.

**Date:** 2026-11-05 · **Auditor:** Devin (independent, zero-trust)

**Governing documents consulted:** `openOODA/rules.oot` (25 laws, esp. §1.19 naming, §1.25 zero-link),
`rfcs/0012-unix-less-is-more-consolidation.oot` (377→129 plan + persona contract), `rfcs/0010` (fleet governance), `rfcs/0011`
(validation), `northstar.oot` (layer model), `docs/tools-backlog.oot` (152-repo backlog table), `zero_symlinks_directive.oot`.

---

## 0. Executive Summary

The fleet is **mid-consolidation and diverged from its own RFC in both directions**: some repos are correctly absorbed and ready
to archive, some RFC-canonical names do not exist at all, several absorptions landed in semantically wrong hosts or in *two
hosts at once*, and at least three absorbed-vs-canonical relationships are **inverted between code and RFC** (`oomail`,
`oocoder`, `oodeploy`/`oobuild-fleet`). Repo/dir names are almost universally consistent with `BIN` names (zero `BIN`≠dir
mismatches found), but ~1 in 6 names are cryptic, colliding, typo'd, or duplicated across layers. The deepest structural issue
is not any single bad name — it is that **most repos are clones of one shared capability-substrate template with 1–5 domain
pages of actual identity**, so "repository count" overstates "product count" roughly 10–20× in L2/L4/L5/L6/L7.

Severity-ordered headline findings:

Table columns: # | Finding | Severity
- F1 | Consolidation direction inverted vs RFC 0012 in ≥3 pairs (oomail→oochat, oocoder→ooreviewer, oodeploy↔oobuild-fleet) |
  **Critical**
- F2 | Same persona embedded in **two different canonical hosts** (oodns-cloud, ooqueue, ooclip, ooshot, ootaskmgr, ooarchitect,
  oohealthd) | **High**
- F3 | RFC-canonical repos that **do not exist**: `oooffice`, `ooauthd`, `ooboot`, `oohal`, `oodrv-{storage,io,virt}`, plus
  doc-named `oocodec`, `ootext`, `oozip`, `oocage` | **High**
- F4 | `oosqllite` is a committed typo (upstream `sqlite3`); `oote` squats the `tee` namespace while meaning "theming engine" |
  **High**
- F5 | Systemic substrate cloning: L4/L5 repos are ~27 identical capability-demo pages + 1 domain page; DE repos `oowm`/`ooterm`
  are file-for-file identical trees | **High**
- F6 | Doc drift: RFC vs `northstar.oot` disagree on canonical sets (L4 especially); repo counts drift (4/5/6 meta, 10/11/9
  core) | **Medium**
- F7 | `oooda-engine` docs advertise persona **symlink** applets (`bin/ooplanner`, `bin/ooarchitect`), contradicting §1.25;
  cloud+agents lack the `link-law` Makefile gate entirely | **Medium**
- F8 | 104 of 258 tools are one identical 13-file "file-grep skeleton" stub whose READMEs all claim "Native X TOOL" — misleading
  product claims at fleet scale | **Medium**
- F9 | Six shipped tools carry a stale `oofzf` `AGENTS.md` (wrong repo name, wrong domain table) | **Low**
- F10 | Workspace debris: `/Projects/openOODA/8` scratch file, `openOODA-DE/bracket_probe*.txt`, 385 `.ooda-cache/` residue dirs
  | **Low**

---

## 1. Method & Evidence Base

- Enumerated every `*/` under all 9 orgs: **378 product subdirectories + 1 stray file + 2 stray probes + 1 docs-site repo** (
  `openOODA-tools.github.io`).
- Per repo, extracted: `README.md` H1/tagline, `Makefile` `BIN`, `PERSONAS`, presence/shape of `persona/` or `personas/`, `.oo`
  file inventory, and "Absorbed" banner markers.
- Verified absorption claims by checking for physical persona subtrees (`<host>/<absorbed>/`, `persona/<name>/`,
  `core/persona/<name>/`) rather than trusting README text.
- Skeleton-stub detection: repos matching the uniform `cli.oo + read.oo + core/{cap,codec,engine,ring,table}.oo` relay template.
- Cross-checked every canonical name in RFC 0012 §2.3 and `northstar.oot` §1.1 against on-disk dirs (ghost-name test).

**Zero `BIN` mismatches:** every Makefile `BIN` matches its directory name (`dist/` in L0+L3, `bin/` elsewhere — a minor
convention inconsistency). **Zero symlinks/hardlinks on disk** (§1.25 holds physically).

---

## 2. Category 1 — Naming Mismatches & Misleading Names

### 2.1 Concrete name defects (repo/binary level)

Table columns: Name | Problem | Evidence
- `oosqllite` | **Typo**: upstream is `sqlite3`; correct form is `oosqlite`. Typo is committed across dir, `BIN`, and
  `AGENTS.md`; its `README.md` is 0 bytes | `tools/oosqllite/`
- `oote` | Squats the `tee` namespace but is the **openOODA Theming Engine** (96 pages). No `ootee` exists; `tee` is uncovered.
  Any reader/batch script reading `oote` will expect `tee(1)` | `tools/oote/README.md`
- `oonaa` | Cryptic acronym ("NO AMBIENT AUTH" capability checker). Undiscoverable | `tools/oonaa/`
- `ooripl` | Cryptic acronym ("INTERACTIVE REPL"); overlaps `oosh`'s REPL role | `tools/ooripl/`
- `oobiew` | Upstream-derived (BIEW hex viewer) but collides visually with DE `ooview`; reads as a typo | `tools/oobiew/`
- `oobound` vs `ooboundary` | Near-identical names, different domains (runtime bounds vs network egress). Indistinguishable |
  `tools/oobound*/`
- `oocsplt` | Truncated `csplit` (expected `oocsplit`); asymmetric vs `oosplit` | `tools/oocsplt/`
- `ooss`/`oossh`/`oossl` | Three one-letter-apart names (`ss`, `ssh`, `openssl`) — maximal typo hazard | tools
- `oood` | `od` octal dumper; reads as `oooda`/`oooda-engine` prefix collision | `tools/oood/`
- `oomeminfo` | Upstream is `free`, but `oofree` also exists — two repos claim the same memory-report domain | tools ×2
- `ootls`/`oossl` | **Both declare upstream `openssl`** — duplicated parity contract for one upstream tool | tools ×2
- `oofilter` | "Native AWK Drop-In" while `ooawk` ("RECORD PROCESSOR") also exists — two AWKs | tools ×2
- `ooimage` | Sounds like an image viewer; is the **OS image builder** (containers, `ooda_buildbox` tiers) | `OS/ooimage/`
- `oomenubar` | DE-concept name living in L3 tools; it is a terminal status bar, overlaps `oopanel` | `tools/oomenubar/`
- `oorev` | `rev` line-reverser; name implies "revision/revocation" in a capability system | `tools/oorev/`
- `openOODA-tools.github.io` | Docs-site repo inside a product org (necessary GitHub convention, but it breaks the `oo*` uniform
  namespace and inflates repo counts in docs: 256/257/258) | tools

### 2.2 Doc-canonical names that don't exist (ghost names)

Referenced as canonical in governing docs or READMEs but **no repo exists**:

- `oooffice` — RFC 0012's L5 office-suite host. The actual suite consolidator is **`oodocs`** (real personas `oosheet`, `oodeck`
  , `oonotes`, `oopdf` implemented under `oodocs/personas/`).
- `ooauthd` — RFC's L2 auth daemon. The actual session/policy merge landed as `oosession` ⊃ `policy/`.
- `ooboot`, `oohal`, `oodrv-storage`, `oodrv-io`, `oodrv-virt` — RFC's L1 canonical set. Reality kept `ookernel-*`/`oodrv-*`
  names and did *different* pairwise absorbs (§4).
- `oocodec`, `ootext` — named in `zero_symlinks_directive.oot` as consolidated canonical tools; the actual repos remain
  `oobase32/58/64` and `oosort`+text family.
- `oozip` — listed in RFC 0012 §2.2.4 archive family; only `oounzip` exists (unzip with no zip producer).
- `oocage` — `ooaid` README names it as the GPU-cell runner tool; no such repo exists anywhere.
- `oowhich`, `ooide`, `oomsg`, `ooidle`, `ooscreensaver` — persona tokens only (legitimate under RFC's "persona is a token, not
  a file" rule, but they inflate the namespace in docs without any marker saying *token-only*).

### 2.3 Layer/role labels that lie

- **`oollama`** README calls itself "Layer 4 (`openOODA-tools`)" — tools is **L3**, not L4; it is also absorbed into `ooaid`
  (**L2**), so a single README spans three wrong/right layer claims.
- Skeleton stubs (104 repos) uniformly claim "**Native X TOOL**" while shipping a file-grep skeleton that relays unknown argv
  upstream. `ooip show <file>` greps a file; it does not run `ip`. The READMEs present vapor capability as shipped product —
  misleading names *of function*.
- `oorealm`/`oosvc-mgr`/`ooimage`/`ooinit` READMEs badge "Layer: `os` on `openOODA-core` (L0)" — the repos live in the L2 OS
  org; the "on L0" phrasing is confusing but internally consistent (built on L0).

### 2.4 Stale `AGENTS.md` copies

Six shipped tools carry `oofzf`'s house-laws file verbatim — wrong repo name, wrong capability matrix (`FsReadCap/EnvCap` only),
wrong domain table (`score/`, `ui/`, `render/`, `ipc/` + `packaging/oofzf.spec`):
`ooclock`, `oocurl`, `oops`, `oosed`, `ootar`, `ootree`.
(`oocurl` literally says "Every agent operating on `oofzf` MUST comply".)

### 2.5 `BIN`/`dist` convention split (minor)

`openOODA-core` and `openOODA-tools` emit to `dist/`; kernel, OS, DE, apps, cloud, agents emit to `bin/`. Not a violation, but
an inconsistent install contract — the Binary Parity Law (`~/.openooda/bin/<tool> == dist/<tool>`) only literally names `dist/`.

---

## 3. Category 2 — Scope Pollution / Single-Responsibility Violations

### 3.1 The systemic one: substrate-clone repos

The dominant SRP violation is architectural, not per-name. Across L2/L4/L5/L6/L7 most "products" are **the same
capability-substrate demo payload** (capability register, Byzantine quorum, Merkle audit chain, blackbox recorder, speculative
transactions, warrant/delegate/revoke, PQC seal) + a thin domain veneer:

- **L5 apps**: every repo ≈ 27 identical pages (`core/{blackbox,capreg,engine,persist,quorum,share,speculate,state}`,
  `sec/{audit,delegate,pqcseal,revoke}`, `ipc/{framebuf,payload,wayland_client}`, 8 `tests/*`, `ui/view.oo`) + **exactly one
  domain page**: `oomail→mbox.oo`, `oonotes→stats.oo`, `oopdf→info.oo`, `oosheet→query.oo`, `oodeck→slides.oo`,
  `oopaint→info.oo` *(same filename as oopdf's)*, `oogit-ui→refs.oo`, `oochat→chatlog.oo`, `oomedia→probe.oo`. ≈90% boilerplate
  / 10% product.
- **L4 DE**: `oowm` and `ooterm` have **file-for-file identical trees** (28 files: same
  `core/{attest,audit,blackbox,cap,codec,consensus,delegate,engine,rect,revoke,ring,share,speculate,table}` + same 8 tests).
  Product identity lives invisibly *inside* `engine.oo`/`table.oo`.
- **L3 stubs**: 104 repos share one 13-file skeleton (`cli.oo`, `read.oo`, `core/{cap,codec,engine,ring,table}.oo`,
  `qa/parity/*`, `tests/test_main.oo`) where the only real verb is "grep this file; relay unknown argv to upstream".
- **L6 cloud**: `ooqueue` vs `ooconsent` share ~28 of ~36 pages; domain difference is ~4 pages each.
- **L2 OS daemons**: uniform `core/{capreg,machine,parse,policy,store,types,warrant,wire}` + `hw/*` skeleton; README one-liners
  ("X table that…") admit they are bounded state machines, not daemons.

**Audit verdict:** the RFC's own diagnosis (1,079 duplicated harness pages) is confirmed and ongoing. These repos violate the
*spirit* of single responsibility: the repo name promises a product; the tree delivers a shared demo substrate. Before any
archive/split decision, decide whether the substrate becomes one shared `std/` import surface (the law already says code sharing
via `import "std/..."`, not copies).

### 3.2 Repo-level multi-domain scope

- **`oofiles`** absorbed `ooview` (doc viewer), `ooedit` (text editor), `ooclip` (clipboard broker), `ooshot` (screenshot),
  `ootaskmgr` (task manager) — a *file manager* now serving five unrelated desktop domains. Legally multi-call; semantically a
  kitchen sink. `ooportal` is the more defensible host for `ooclip`/`ooshot` (FrameCap-gated) — and indeed **also** embeds them
  (see F2).
- **`oocockpit`** (absorbed into `oopanel`) embeds `ooagent-hud` (RFC-canonical!) + `ootaskmgr` — a nested shell-inside-a-shell
  carrying a canonical product.
- **`oochat`** carries a fuller mail implementation (`personas/oomail/{engine,mbox,persist}`) than the standalone `oomail` repo
  (`mbox.oo` only). Two divergent mail impls exist; the non-RFC one is bigger.
- **`oocat`** — "file viewer and pager" = `cat` + `less` + syntax highlighting; triples its Unix mandate.
- **`ooclock`** — TUI matrix clock + stopwatch + pomodoro; three domains.
- **`ooenv`** — env controller + secret sanitizer + exec simulator.
- **`ooaid`** — inference daemon that also absorbed the `oollama` CLI client (L3 tool inside an L2 daemon — cross-layer scope,
  though defensible as client+server pair) and references nonexistent `oocage`.
- **Kernel pairwise absorbs**: `oodrv-net` ⊃ `oodrv-pci`+`oodrv-usb-hid` personas (a *network* driver serving PCI-bus and
  USB-HID personas); `ookernel-sched` ⊃ `ookernel-mm`+`ookernel-ipc` (scheduler serving MM+IPC). Technically fine as personas,
  semantically arbitrary — the RFC's `oodrv-{storage,io,virt}` grouping is the correct domain shape and was not followed.
- **`oomedia`** — the user's example is **verified remediated**: README documents the flasher was extracted to `ooflash` (real
  17-page dedicated repo with devlock/digest/rawio/verify); zero flash/usb residue in `oomedia`. *But* `ooflash` is absent from
  RFC 0012's L5 canonical list — the doc never caught up to the correct split.

### 3.3 Org-boundary (cross-layer) leaks

- `omenubar` (terminal status bar — a DE chrome concept) sits in tools while `oopanel`/`ooborder` cover the same visual role.
- `ooherdr` (agents) ships an `oomux` persona — terminal multiplexer while `ootmux`+`ooscreen` exist in tools.
- `mcp` naming appears at three layers: `core/mcp` (server), `tools/oomcp` (gateway/router), `tools/oomcprun` (task runner).
- DNS exists at three layers: `tools/oodns` (resolver), `OS/oodnsd` (caching resolver), `cloud/oodns-cloud` (DNS service) — plus
  3 more lookup clients `oodig`/`oohost`/`oonslookup` in tools.
- Sessions: `OS/oosession`, `tools/oologinctl` (upstream: loginctl — same domain), `tools/ooscreen`+`ootmux` (session detach).
- Power: `tools/{oopower,ooreboot,ooshutdown}` vs `OS/oopowerd`.
- `oomenubar`, `oonotify` vs `DE/ootoast`; `tools/oofile` vs `DE/oofiles` vs `OS/oofs-*`; `tools/ooguard` vs `agents/oogovern`.
